    'intro'  => '<p>Cambridge sits across the Charles River from Boston and is home to <strong>Harvard University</strong> and the <strong>Massachusetts Institute of Technology</strong>. Kendall Square, next to MIT, is one of the world&rsquo;s densest clusters of biotech and tech companies, so the city draws a constant flow of visiting researchers, postdocs, summer interns and people relocating for lab jobs.</p><p>The Boston area is known for its <strong>September 1</strong> lease cycle: a huge share of apartments turn over on that date, and finding a lease that starts at any other time can be hard. Short-term sublets fill the gap. Students leaving after spring term sublet their rooms from late May to August, and summer sublets give interns and researchers a furnished bridge until a September lease begins.</p><p>Housing in Cambridge is expensive and competitive. Older triple-deckers and brick walk-ups dominate, so ask about laundry, air conditioning and stairs. Being close to a Red Line station is usually worth more than an extra few hundred square feet.</p>',
